course: dictionary
lesson: 4783
title: Hugely: a word that adds strength
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: «Hugely» is an adverb. It tells you how much or how strongly something happens, and it works like a volume knob turned up high. «Hugely popular» means very popular.
explain: Try swapping in a simple word to check your meaning. «She was hugely relieved.» You cannot measure relief with a ruler, so «hugely» shows the feeling is very strong. «Very relieved» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: «Hugely» does not give an exact number. «Pay varies hugely» means pay is very different from one job to another. It does not tell you how much anyone earns.
fact: «Hugely» means very much, or in a very big way.
fact: «Hugely popular» means very popular, and «hugely important» means very important.
fact: «Hugely» shows how big a feeling or change is, not an exact number.
fact: «Pay varies hugely»: a carer may get about £11 an hour, while a top footballer can earn a million or more.
fact: «A pterosaur's wing was skin stretched from one hugely long fourth finger.» Here «hugely» makes long even stronger: the finger was very long.
exercise: «The calls matter hugely to Baba.» What does hugely mean here? | Quietly | A little bit | *Very much | Once in a while
exercise: «Space missions are hugely hard and costly.» What does hugely tell us? | *They are very hard and costly | They are hard only sometimes | They have one set price | They never finish
exercise: Does «hugely» give an exact number? | Yes, it always gives a number | Yes, it counts the money | Only in maths lessons | *No, it shows how much, not a number
test: «Lemmings' numbers rise and fall hugely.» What does hugely suggest? | *They change by a very large amount | They never move | They go up by one each year | They stay exactly the same
test: «She was hugely relieved.» Which word means nearly the same as hugely here? | slightly | quietly | *very | never
exam: Which sentence uses hugely to show a very strong feeling? | «He counted ten sweets.» | «The rain stopped at three.» | *«She was hugely relieved when the bus came.» | «The bus was yellow.»
